  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Plenty of sunshine with comfortable humidity \ntoday & Sat and highs in the 70s. Lows tonight in the 40s & 50s.\n\nLarge high pressure builds over the region today into Sat. This will \nresult in plenty of sunshine and very pleasant September weather \nwith comfortable humidity. Highs today and Sat will be well into the \n70s with much less wind than yesterday. Mainly clear skies, light \nwinds, and a dry airmass in place will result in ideal radiational \ncooling conditions tonight. Low temps tonight will bottom out in the \n40s to the lower 50s...But middle to upper 50s in the urban centers \nof downtown Boston/Providence. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and possibly a few thunderstorms with brief \ntorrential rainfall possible late Sat night into the first half of \nSun. Low probability for localized flash flooding. \n\nHigh pressure moves off the coast for the second half of the \nweekend. At the same time...a shortwave and an associated warm front \nwill approach from the southwest. This will result in quite the mid \nlevel instability burst. Showalter indices drop nearly 20 units to \nnear or below zero inside about 12 hours. That rapid change in \nstability supports the development of a complex of showers and \nperhaps a few thunderstorms. This coupled with Pwats rising to \naround 2 inches support the potential for pockets of torrential \nrainfall. This will be dependent on where a meso-surface low tracks \nand sets up a boundary for enhanced convergence. This will of course \nbe a nowcast/mesoscale type of forecast...but given the Pwat \nplume/low level boundary there is a low probability for localized \nflash flooding. Usually for this to occur...several things need to \nhappen on the mesoscale but it is something we need to watch with \nthe main concern near and south of I-90.\n\nThe timing still needs to be refined...but favoring late Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning as to when the bulk of the rainfall \noccurs. Rainfall amounts are highly uncertain and potentially \nextremely variable. Perhaps just a tenth to half inch in many \nlocations...but the potential exists for a very localized narrow \nband of 2-3\"+ of rainfall in a short amount of time. The models that \nindicate this risk have it in a different locations too. And even if \nthis occurs it may not end up in our forecast area. Nonetheless...it \nis something we certainly need to watch. It will be also become \nquite muggy by Sunday afternoon with highs in the 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry and seasonable Mon-Tue but a brief bout of \nsummer warmth and humidity possible Wed and/or Thu ahead of a cold \nfront.\n\nHigh pressure builds back into the region Mon and Tue bringing dry \nand seasonable weather. High temps will mainly be in the upper 60s \nto the middle 70s with lows in the 40s and 50s. This high pressure \nsystem moves east of the region by the middle of next week. This may \nallow for a brief return of summerlike warmth/humidity sometime Wed \nand/or Thu.",
